      "role": "hook",
      "headline": "Rome ran an empire of millions with a surprisingly small staff."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.075.c2",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Governors and taxes",
      "body": "Augustus split the provinces: peaceful ones had governors chosen by the Senate, while frontier provinces with armies were run by his own deputies. Provinces paid taxes on land and on people, and a census counted them for taxation."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.075.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Local help",
      "body": "Rome ran its provinces with few officials, relying on local elites to govern their own cities and collect taxes. In the Republic, tax collection had often been contracted out to private companies of publicani, who were notorious for abuses.",
      "callout": "Publicani: private tax contractors"
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.075.c4",
      "type": "fact",
      "fact": "Pontius Pilate was a provincial governor.",
      "context": "He was the Roman prefect who governed Judaea from 26 to 36 CE."
    },
    {
